#!/bin/sh
# script/apropos: search every document in this repository and say where each one lives.
#
#   script/apropos capability          what this tree says about capabilities, strongest first
#   script/apropos syscall             ... and where the syscall numbers actually are
#
# Milestone 40 built a documentation service for the *guest*: an inverted index over markdown, a
# reader that merges shards, and an `apropos` builtin at the nife prompt. That index was pointed at
# the six pages the filesystem image installs, because a guest can only search what it holds. A
# person with a checkout has no such limit, so this points the same code at all of it: 512 pages,
# five megabytes, every note, decision, roadmap block and repository-root document, plus every
# crate's and program's own module header.
#
# **Why it exists**, and it is a finding rather than a nicety. Milestone 117 has run three strangers
# at this tree with no help. What none of them could do is on the record in notes/stranger-test.md:
# reach notes/net.md, notes/capabilities.md or ANY design/decisions/ file by following the tree
# while doing ordinary work, or find crates/abi/src/lib.rs, which is four syscall numbers and the
# whole design on one screen. None of those is hidden; nothing a person would type led to them.
# This is the thing to type. It is also why the crate and program headers are indexed: the document
# a reader wants about `abi` *is* that file's header, and no markdown page was ever going to fix it.
#
# It is the same index and the same reader as the guest's, on purpose (`manual::index::build` and
# `manual::index::search`), so a defect in the layout shows up in both places and a fix lands in
# both. What differs is what a result names: at the nife prompt a result names a page in the store
# you can open there, and here it names a path in this repository.
#
# BUGS
#
# - **Whole words only, and no stemming.** `syscall` and `syscalls` are two terms, so a search for
#   one does not find the other. `manual::index::normalize` is what decides that and it is the
#   guest's tokeniser, which has to run with no allocator.
# - **Underscores join and hyphens do not.** `apropos line_editor` works, because the builder writes
#   the joined term as well as the halves; `apropos line-discipline` does not, because joining
#   across a hyphen would manufacture a term out of every hyphenated phrase in the corpus. A path is
#   not searchable either: `notes/glob.md` folds to `notesglobmd`, which no page says.
# - **One word per search.** There is no phrase query and no intersection of two terms.
# - **Ranking is occurrence count and nothing else**, so a long page mentioning a word in passing
#   can outrank a short page about it. See `crates/manual/src/index.rs`'s BUGS.
# - **Sixteen results, always**, because that is the guest reader's fixed table
#   (`manual::index::RESULTS_MAX`) and this uses the guest reader. The line under the results says
#   how many pages matched in total.
# - **It rebuilds the whole index on every run** (about a second). There is no cache, because a
#   cache that can be stale is worse here than a second of work.
# - **The first stranger it was built for never ran it, and had the name in front of it three
#   times.** Milestone 117's run 5 (2026-08-18) is the first onboarding run against a tree
#   containing this script, and this script exists because three earlier runs could not reach
#   `notes/net.md`, `notes/capabilities.md`, any `design/decisions/` file, or
#   `crates/abi/src/lib.rs`. Run 5 listed `script/`, so `apropos` was the first name in that
#   listing; it read the guest's `apropos` builtin in `crates/swish/src/lib.rs`; and it read the
#   `apropos photosynthesis` line in `SHELL_CHECK_SCRIPT`. It ran neither this nor anything like
#   it, reached no `design/decisions/` file and never opened `notes/net.md`, which is the exact
#   gap this was written to close. **The reason is placement, not the tool**: the only page that
#   says what this does is `notes/scripts.md`, and no stranger in five runs has opened it. A
#   search tool nobody is told about is a search tool nobody uses, and the honest reading is that
#   this is currently written for people who already know the tree. Recorded rather than fixed,
#   because where it should be named is a claim about the reading order and those are calef's.
#
# Name: unrecorded. Provisional, minted by milestone 40's lane on 2026-08-18 and not put to calef.
# It is deliberately the same word as the builtin at the nife prompt, which is the claim: one verb,
# one index, two places you can be standing. `script/search` and `script/docs` were the
# alternatives and both lose that; `apropos` also carries the Unix meaning a reader already has,
# which AGENTS.md's naming rule says is the best name available.

set -e
cd "$(dirname "$0")/.."
exec cargo xtask apropos "$@"
